---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="How Not to Be Wrong | Library | Amitoj Singh" description="Jordan Ellenberg on mathematics as extended common sense—a science of not being wrong hammered out by centuries of hard work.">
  <article class="container-zen section-zen">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <a href="/library" class="text-ink-muted hover:text-ink mb-8 inline-block">&larr; Back to Library</a>

      <h1 class="mb-2 text-balance">How Not to Be Wrong: The Power of Mathematical Thinking</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-ink-muted mb-6">Jordan Ellenberg</p>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-sm text-ink-muted mb-8">
        <span>8-12 hours</span>
        <span class="text-oat-400">&middot;</span>
        <span>Accessible</span>
        <span class="text-oat-400">&middot;</span>
        <span>Mathematics, Philosophy, Systems</span>
      </div>

      <div class="accent-line mb-8"></div>

      <!-- WHY I RECOMMEND THIS -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Why I Recommend This</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg">
          <p>Teaching systems thinking at a business school surfaces a recurring problem: students can run regression models but can't tell when the output is nonsense. They've learned calculation without developing judgment about when the calculation makes sense. Ellenberg's book addresses this gap directly—not by teaching more techniques, but by showing how mathematical thinking extends common sense rather than replacing it.</p>
          <p>Working at the intersection of technology and organizations, I keep encountering the same pattern: we apply linear thinking to nonlinear problems, mistake correlation for causation, and trust statistical significance without questioning the underlying assumptions. This book provides the conceptual vocabulary for recognizing these errors—survivorship bias, regression to the mean, the prosecutor's fallacy—and demonstrates that mathematical humility (knowing what you can't calculate) matters as much as mathematical skill.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- THE BOOK -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">The Book</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg">
          <p>Ellenberg argues that mathematics is "the extension of common sense by other means"—not a specialized skill reserved for prodigies, but a systematic way of not being wrong refined through centuries of collective effort. The book challenges the dominant educational paradigm where mathematics appears as rote memorization, showing instead that mathematical thinking is an essential tool for navigating a world saturated with data, shaped by algorithms, and characterized by nonlinear relationships.</p>
          <p>Through five domains (linearity, inference, expectation, regression, and existence), Ellenberg demonstrates how mathematical thinking reveals hidden structures beneath surface complexity. He covers survivorship bias using WWII bomber armor placement, the replication crisis in psychology, MIT students who beat the Massachusetts lottery through expected value calculations, and Arrow's impossibility theorem proving that perfect voting systems cannot exist. The book's lasting contribution lies in repositioning mathematics itself—from technical specialization to civic capability, from pure calculation to augmented judgment.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- PASSAGES -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Passages That Stayed With Me</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"Mathematics is the extension of common sense by other means."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">The core thesis—mathematics isn't alien to human thinking but a natural amplification of it.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"The armour doesn't go where the bullet holes are. It goes where the bullet holes aren't."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Abraham Wald's WWII insight about bomber armor—the definitive illustration of survivorship bias. The missing bullet holes were on the missing planes.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"Working an integral or performing a linear regression is something a computer can do quite effectively. Understanding whether the result makes sense—or deciding whether the method is the right one to use in the first place—requires a guiding human hand."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Distinguishing calculation from thinking—why human judgment remains essential in an age of powerful computation.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"One of the most painful parts of teaching mathematics is seeing students damaged by the cult of the genius. The genius cult tells students it's not worth doing mathematics unless you're the best at mathematics, because those special few are the only ones whose contributions matter."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Challenging the myth that mathematical thinking belongs only to prodigies—a call for democratizing quantitative literacy.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"It's not enough that the data be consistent with your theory; they have to be inconsistent with the negation of your theory, the dreaded null hypothesis."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">The rigorous standard for claiming discovery—statistical significance is the beginning of inquiry, not the end.</p>
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- READ THIS IF -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Read This If...</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-ink-light">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You make decisions based on data but weren't trained in statistics—this book provides the conceptual toolkit for avoiding systematic errors</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You suspect that "common sense" often misleads when dealing with probability, correlation, or trend extrapolation</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You want to understand the replication crisis, p-hacking, and why so many published research findings turn out to be wrong</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You believe mathematical thinking is only for specialists and want to see why it's actually extended common sense available to everyone</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You need to evaluate policy proposals, business strategies, or media claims that rely on quantitative arguments</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- SKIP THIS IF -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Skip This If...</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-ink-light">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-oat-400">&bull;</span>
            <span>You already have formal training in statistics and probability theory—the concepts will be familiar (though the examples might still delight)</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-oat-400">&bull;</span>
            <span>You want deeper coverage of computational methods, network mathematics, or algorithmic bias—this focuses on foundational reasoning patterns</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div class="pt-8 border-t border-oat-300">
        <a href="/library" class="text-sage-700 hover:text-sage-900 font-medium">&larr; Back to Library</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose p {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }
</style>
